const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Scientific Editing","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/scientific-editing/","description":"Manuscript editing read against the reporting guideline your design requires, at its current version. Three editors. From $0.035 a word. Free guideline check.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is scientific editing, and how is it different from language editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Language editing makes the English right. Scientific editing does that and reads the manuscript against the reporting guideline its design requires — a different activity performed by a different person.\n\nThe guideline is not optional background: CONSORT 2025 for a randomised trial, PRISMA 2020 for a systematic review, STROBE for an observational study, ARRIVE 2.0 for animal research, TRIPOD+AI for a prediction model, CHEERS 2022 for a health economic evaluation, STARD 2015 for diagnostic accuracy, CARE for a case report, SQUIRE 2.0 for quality improvement, COREQ or SRQR for qualitative work. The EQUATOR library lists 707 guidelines, which is why matching the right one to your design is a step rather than an assumption."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will editing increase my chances of acceptance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Editing removes obstacles to a fair reading. It does not make a study more likely to be true.\n\nWhat it can honestly do is specific and checkable: make sure the manuscript reports what the current guideline asks it to report; bring it inside the word limit; make sure the English is not in the way; and surface the questions — a missing registered outcome, two figures that disagree — while you can still answer them.\n\nOn the sample published here, twenty-six CONSORT items were present and seven absent or partial. The study was fine. The manuscript did not show it. That gap is what editing closes — not the same as making the paper better."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which reporting guideline applies to my study, and is my version current?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Use the checker at the top of this page, and check the version — several changed recently and templates do not.\n\nCONSORT 2025 and SPIRIT 2025 were published in April 2025, superseding CONSORT 2010 and SPIRIT 2013 — a checklist saved before that date is superseded, and this is the commonest reporting failure we see. TRIPOD+AI, BMJ 2024, is now the one statement covering prediction models using regression or machine learning. PRISMA 2020 is still current — emphatically, because “PRISMA 2026” circulates widely with no primary source behind it. STROBE 2007 is also still current.\n\nOne disclosure: EQUATOR gives January 2025 for CONSORT while the journal records give April. We use April and flag it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My paper was desk-rejected. Is that an editing problem?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, yes — and this is where editing is worth the most, because a desk rejection is usually a reporting or fit failure rather than a scientific one.\n\nThree things produce most of them. Scope: the journal does not publish work like this, which no amount of editing fixes. Format: over the word limit, wrong structure, missing required sections. Reporting: a screening editor cannot confirm quickly that the study is methodologically adequate, usually because an item the guideline asks for is absent.\n\nSend the decision letter. A desk rejection and a rejection after review are different diagnoses. An editor working without it is guessing at a problem somebody has already described."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What will you not change in my manuscript?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your data, your results and your conclusions. Expect four refusals.\n\nWhere two numbers disagree, you get a query, not a reconciliation — an editor who picks one has invented a fact that will be in your published paper. Where a conclusion outruns the results, you get it quoted back with the relevant result beside it, not softened; it would then be our finding. Where a section needs writing rather than editing you get a recommendation and not the text. And where a reference does not support the sentence citing it, you get a flag.\n\nOn the sample published here that produced nineteen queries, none answered on the authors’ behalf."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you edit my thesis, or my clinical study report?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not at this level, and the reason differs for each.\n\nA thesis is bound by guidelines restricting editing to copyediting and proofreading: the IPEd guidelines, July 2025, say editors “should not make corrections to the content, substance or structure of the thesis”. The substantive and developmental levels sold on this page are what a thesis editor must not do. Thesis work is bounded separately on our thesis editing page.\n\nA clinical study report or clinical evaluation report answers to an ICH guideline or an MDR annex — handled on our regulatory writing page. One boundary the other way: a thesis chapter turned into a journal article stops being a thesis, and substantive editing becomes permissible."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/scientific-editing/"}]}];

